= Source lineage =

This page defines the authority and editorial lineage used to build BITwiki V2.

== Source authority ==

For inherited BITwiki material:

# '''Exact public V1 page/revision source''' remains read-only under <code>archive-v1/</code> after completion of the content-level reconciliation and is anchored to immutable snapshot commit <code>3100db1d7807b752e41b6ccf7f88498982cfb2d4</code>; final per-source status is recorded in <code>docs/v1-reconciliation-ledger.json</code>. Working-tree retirement remains a separate operation.
# '''Current first-party public BITwiki / BIThub / BITCORE / GitHub material''' where it clarifies later doctrine or current operation.
# '''Earlier public writings and historical design artifacts''' used to recover intellectual lineage.
# '''Historical Notion/design material''' used as archaeology and provenance, not automatic current-state truth.
# '''New V2 editorial connective text''' only where the source corpus does not already provide a better formulation.

== Historical V1 snapshot coverage ==

The archival snapshot of the former public V1 corpus was verified at capture time against the then-readable wiki. These figures describe that historical capture, not the current live V2 corpus:

* 186 / 186 public pages;
* 606 / 606 public revision bodies;
* all current page IDs and revision IDs;
* all 162 used categories and complete memberships;
* all 9 implemented templates and callers;
* File revision metadata;
* namespace and maintenance-report state.

The capture-time fidelity check was reported as matching the then-readable source. This is historical audit evidence, not a check reproduced in this repository checkout or proof of the current live deployment.

This does '''not''' mean every body of every page ever deleted from V1 is publicly recoverable. The deletion log contains historical deletions whose bodies require privileged deleted-revision access or older backups. The tracked deletion records <code>v1-deleted-content-audit.json</code> and <code>v1-deleted-unresolved.json</code> preserve that limitation. They do not establish recovery of inaccessible deleted bodies.

== Editorial rule ==

'''Canonicalization is not paraphrasing.'''

For foundational, constitutional, philosophical, scientific, and other authored material:

# recover the exact source first;
# compare its public revisions and parallel versions;
# identify strong language, unique distinctions, citations, metaphors, and implementation assumptions;
# distinguish current canon from historical branch;
# preserve provenance for merges, rewrites, redirects, and retirement;
# write new connective prose only where needed.

A cleaner sentence is not automatically a better sentence. An unusual sentence is not automatically noise.

== Design intent versus implementation ==

V1 contains sophisticated documentation for templates, properties, categories, concepts, modules, validation systems, and epistemic machinery that was not always instantiated as a live MediaWiki object.

Therefore:

<pre>
design writing ≠ implemented namespace object
wanted object ≠ implemented object
category membership ≠ authored Category page
semantic annotation ≠ authored Property page
specification ≠ deployed capability
</pre>

Both layers remain evidence.

== Historical material ==

Older material can be obsolete in implementation while remaining important in thought. Newer is not automatically better. Older is not automatically canonical.

Foundational writings may have genuine branches. The former Charter lineage comprises an epistemic/field Charter, a research-space Charter, and a performative Holographic Charter. These texts are independent historical sources, not present constitutional authorities. The [[BITwiki:Constitution|approved Constitution]] is BITwiki's sole founding instrument; the [[BITwiki:Foundations|Foundations index]] identifies its companion philosophical writings.

The historical originals and their revision bodies remain recoverable at snapshot commit <code>3100db1d7807b752e41b6ccf7f88498982cfb2d4</code> under <code>archive-v1/pages/Main/</code> and <code>archive-v1/history/Main/</code> for <code>BITwiki Charter</code>, <code>BITwiki Charter Revised</code>, and <code>Charter</code>. The distinctive H=1/symbiosis theory is now examined critically in [[BITwiki:Human-AI symbiosis]]; its use does not retroactively endorse the historical Charter's metaphysical or institutional claims.

The completed 2026 concept-wave working records and V1 migration analyses are process evidence. Retiring their project-namespace renderings must not erase authorship, revision history, reproducible classification evidence, or any unresolved issue transferred to the active knowledge request and validation work.

See [[BITwiki:Foundations]].

== Assistant-authored August 2026 draft ==

The Git history between the original repository baseline and the source-grounded rebuild records an assistant-authored experimental V2 draft. It remains useful as an audit trail and source of proposed structure, but its prose is not inherited project canon merely because it appeared in Git.

No Git history is erased; current canonical source is corrected while previous commits remain recoverable.

== Canonicalization loop ==

<pre>
raw source
→ context and lineage
→ compare revisions / competing formulations
→ research and cross-check
→ preserve signal
→ remove accidental duplication / obsolete machinery
→ revise
→ verify
→ canonicalize
→ transclude / relate / redirect deliberately
→ publish
</pre>

